    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Have you struggled with staying organized and focused throughout your life, or is this a recent change for you?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, well, you know, my husband asked me that very same thing just the other day when I lost his prescription slip again. He thought maybe it was just my age, you know, being seventy and all, but I... I really don't think it's just getting older. If I'm being completely honest, and I've been thinking about this a lot lately, it's always been this way. Always. I mean, going all the way back to when I was a little girl in grade school. \n\nMy mother kept all my old report cards in this beautiful cedar chest—which I still have, actually, it's sitting at the foot of the guest bed right now, though it's completely covered in laundry I haven't folded yet—but anyway, I was looking through those old report cards a few years back. And every single year, the teachers would write the exact same thing in the margins. They'd say I was a bright girl, very sweet, but just so careless. That was the word they always used, careless. I was constantly forgetting my textbooks at school, or I'd leave my homework on the kitchen counter after I'd spent all evening agonizing over it. \n\nI remember Mrs. Gable—she was my third-grade teacher, such a strict woman with these thick horn-rimmed glasses—she wrote a whole long note to my parents about how she constantly had to stand over my desk and give me reminders to finish my seatwork. Because if she didn't, I'd just stare out the window at the trees or daydream about whatever came into my head, and she wouldn't let me go outside for recess until the worksheet was done. So I'd be stuck inside while everyone else was out playing jump rope or tag. It was just awful. It's like my brain has always been a little bit like a butterfly, just fluttering away from whatever I'm supposed to be doing. So no, it's not new at all, it's just... maybe it feels heavier now because there's so much more paperwork in life, you know? When you're a child, someone else manages the big things, but now it's all on me, and I just can't seem to keep up with it.",
